WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:06.900
The seemingly endless bayous of southern
Louisiana, thousands of square miles of

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:12.420
marshlands, home to gators, birds and not
much else.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.160
This area could not be more inhospitable.

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:24.120
This is not a place that is inclined toward
sustaining human life.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:37.140
Yet. Despite this, a satellite image taken on
January ten, 2019 has uncovered mysterious

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.080
evidence of human activity in these swamps.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.610
Looking at the image, I'm seeing two
different structures here.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.920
To the right, there's sort of a string.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.870
Of boxes or blocks.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:57.730
The other structure is really unusual and
kind of surprising.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.010
It looks like the top of a.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:03.120
Rook, like a giant chess piece in the water.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.240
It's unnerving to think about what's missing
from this picture.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.640
There could be an entire town under the
water.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:14.330
What happened here?

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:25.690
What's drawn, Martin Morgan, to this site is
that officially there has never been a town

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.070
in these marshlands.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.470
But clues suggest otherwise.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.970
That's not natural.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.610
You can totally tell it's a road.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.930
Look at that. This is a bit eerie.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:01.760
The abandoned road leads Morgan to the set of
structures at the eastern side of the river

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:02.360
mouth.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.190
It's just up ahead here.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.560
This thing is incredible.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.210
It's even bigger than it looked in the
satellite photography.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.450
A line of concrete ruins stretches for some
300 yards along the shore.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:33.290
Behind them are concealed, hundreds of poles
driven deep into the lakebed.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.070
This was a massive facility.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.550
Somebody with money, lots of it paid to have
this built.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.040
That suggests to me government money.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.650
Morgan thinks the structures bear the
hallmarks of a secret military installation.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:05.360
Judging by the age, these could be seven or
eight decades old that's dropping all of this

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.970
right into the time period of the Second
World War.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.770
This thing is amazing. Look at this.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:27.030
While much of the structure has long since
rotted away or been destroyed by hurricanes.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.980
Clues to what drew the military into these
swamps do remain.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.670
All down the line of these platforms.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.800
There are iron rings.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.170
These are gun mounts.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:53.350
The fact that you've got all these gun
platforms lined up like this, it makes me

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:55.330
think that I might be looking at.

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:58.940
An anti-aircraft training center.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:07.900
Declassified military records confirm that
these sinking ruins were once a secret World

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.350
War Two training base called Shell Beach.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:17.420
And it played a key role in tackling a
catastrophic shortcoming in America's

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.090
defenses at the dawn of World War Two.

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:29.620
During World War Two, The American forces,
particularly the Navy, had this sort of sense

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:30.910
of invincibility.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:37.480
Now, that is an image that's vastly
different to what the state of the US

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.320
military was, just as the country was
entering World War Two.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:49.010
When Japanese fighters and dive bombers
devastate Pearl Harbor.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.780
Us. Battleships are ill prepared to defend
themselves.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.050
It's a mistake the Navy vows never to
repeat.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.100
When we get involved in the war.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:12.940
After Pearl Harbor, the US military has to
begin a crash training program to get sailors

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:15.670
trained up to shoot down enemy airplanes.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:21.760
With Nazi U-boats and planes also threatening
America's shores.

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:27.820
The Navy starts transporting rookie gunners
into the Louisiana swamp to teach them the

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:28.780
art of anti.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:35.570
Shooting across the lake would have provided
a nice field of fire.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.240
And pilots would actually fly target planes
to be shot at.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.770
Get practice against live targets.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:53.030
40% of the recruits are volunteers, many of
them fresh from high school.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.520
After just eight weeks, they are sent to
fight fascism in Europe.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:07.650
Or to join the Pacific Fleet and experience
the hell of naval combat.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:17.420
Men who trained here would ultimately be on
board ships that are fighting off waves of

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.640
Japanese suicide aircraft.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:28.700
During the first few months of the war, US
anti aircraft gunners shoot down 56 enemy

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:34.680
planes. Within three years, they are
bringing down almost 20 times that number.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.140
And thanks in no small part to these weird
ruins in the Louisiana bayous.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.710
Just think about how critical this was.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:50.300
I mean, in many ways it was facilities like
this that produced the final victory in the

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.230
Second World War.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:03.750
The decaying World War Two facility sits just
400 yards from the larger sunken structure in

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:04.500
the image.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.410
It could be a barracks for the training
center, but that doesn't make any sense.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:11.940
How would you move people back and forth?

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.410
Up close, the mystery deepens.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:22.670
Look at this thing. This looks like what a
castle I drew as a kid Looked like.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.960
If we were in Europe, I'd go, That's
definitely a fort.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.240
But we're not in Europe.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:30.320
We're in Louisiana.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:37.870
There are other forts on the US's southern
coastline dating from the Civil War.

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:44.150
But this is unlike any of them.

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:46.660
The forts are very low profile.

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:50.890
But this thing, it's just looming up out of
the marsh.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:57.760
It's incredible. To really get to the bottom
of what this structure is, I'm going to have

00:07:57.760 --> 00:07:59.440
to get on shore and walk around it.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:06.920
To Morgan. The structure's medieval design is
unprecedented.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:23.110
I mean, it's like a cathedral in here.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:30.640
I have never seen anything like this.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:32.580
This is unique. This is one of a kind.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:41.310
The crumbling walls suggest the swamp fort
likely dates from the early 1800s.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:47.640
The structure clearly belongs to the era
before the introduction of rifle artillery,

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.580
because in the era of rifle artillery, this
thing would not have stood a chance.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.730
The middle of the 19th century.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.550
Witnesses the arrival of rifled barrels.

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:05.020
Revolutionizing the power range and accuracy
of artillery.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:12.900
Brick fortifications are replaced with low
profile concrete ones which can better

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:17.190
withstand the apocalyptic onslaught of this
new generation of weapons.

